  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 234 PM EDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\n- Chance for Isolated Thunderstorms This afternoon and evening\n\nLow stratocu continues to remain through the southern tier of \nMichigan with light southerly winds. Severe weather through\nIndianacontinues to track to the south east with any outflow \nmoving northward running into a wall of stability. That stability \nwill stifle any convection this afternoon and evening through \nsouthern Michigan. The sole caveat will be convection from \nboundary interactions. Currently there is three different outflow \nboundaries moving in different directions that are set to \nintersect through southwest MI. There are some CAMS that suggest \nthat some convection could form. SFC based CAPE is around 1000 \nJ/KG along the I 94 corridor. However, there is upwards of 200 \nJ/KG of CIN. So while it is possible that convection could form \nthrough the south and will need to be watched, the stable air \ncould preclude storm development. \n\n The better chance will be as a speed max moves from the east to southeast across \ncentral lower MI. Lapse rates in the tri city area are 6 to 7 C/km\nthrough the mid levels. Couple that with MU CAPE around 1400 J/KG and PWATS of 1.43\ninches and you have a decent environment for storm development. \n  There is some cu development through central and norther lower. \nHowever, a large cirrus shield should limit daytime heating, \nthough Max temps along the US 10 corridor could get into the mid \n80s. Latest CAMS continue to suggest the potential for isolated \nstorms to be possible after 20Z. DCAPES are in the 800 to 900 J/KG\nrange so Damaging winds are the main concern. Any shower and \nstorm chances will dwindle through the evening hours, \n\n- Foggy and chances for light rainfall through the south to end the work week\n\n  Any rainfall and convection will add to the moisture throughout the lower\nlevels through southern Michigan. Calm winds, a stable air mass and moist\nlower levels is a great recipe for another round of fog through Thursday morning. \n Expect areas of fog to develop overnight. Fog could be denser than this morning's\nbut will hold off on any advisories for now. \n Another upper level trough will drop down from the west \ntomorrow. The track of that trough is trending towards this week's\nprevious systems. They are trending towards IN south into Ohio. \nThe only precipitation could be on the fringes along the I 94 \ncorridor. Only light rain is expected with a low chance for any \nthunder.\n\n- Precipitation Chances This Weekend\n\nThe next chance for widespread precipitation will be Saturday \nnight and Sunday as the pattern shifts and the zonal flow shifts\nnorthward bringing a mid level shortwave propagates over high\npressure building into the Great Plains. \n That shot wave will bring decent surge of moisture Sunday. \n There is some question on timing an position of the mid level \ntrough. However there should be good thickness advection with \nMUCAPE values in excess of 2000 J/KG. So there is the persistent \ntrend for the potential for stronger storms. Mid to long range \nmodels also continue to show a 35 to 40 kt 850mb jet through Lower\nMichigan Sunday morning. This will aid storm development Sunday.",
